antislop-marketing includes material derived from two MIT-licensed projects.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
adenaufal/anti-slop-writing
https://github.com/adenaufal/anti-slop-writing

Supplied the structural rules in references/core.md, the per-model
fingerprints, and the tone-tier concept taken from its Indonesian pack.
Adapted throughout.

Deliberately not carried over:

  - The anti-detector group (T-1 through T-5). This project is not trying to
    defeat Turnitin or GPTZero, and rules aimed at that goal ban vocabulary a
    marketing document legitimately needs.
  - The deliberate-imperfection rules, including permitted comma splices.
    A client deck should not contain manufactured errors.
  - Every statistic it reported without a citable source. The upstream text
    cites a corpus analysis with no link; this repo states directions rather
    than multipliers it cannot stand behind.
  - The 17-to-23-word sentence band. Sentence length is not the tell;
    sameness of shape is, and same_shape_run measures that instead.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
blader/humanizer
https://github.com/blader/humanizer

Supplied the do-not-flag list and the signs-of-human-writing list, both in
references/false-positives.md. Adapted and translated.

One entry was added that the source had no reason to need: a term in a
language pack's loanword list is never reported. A Vietnamese marketing
report is full of English metric names, and flagging them all would make the
tool unusable on the first document it saw.

